# Line endings, pinned where a carriage return breaks a file instead of merely looking odd.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is a PER-MACHINE git setting, so without this file the bytes your clone receives
# depend on how your git is configured rather than on what this repository stores. Every path below
# is stored here with LF; on a machine where `core.autocrlf` is enabled — the default of Git for
# Windows — a checkout rewrites them to CRLF, and the joining kit stops working before it does
# anything:
#
#     $ bash deploy/hw_probe.sh
#     set: usage: set [-abefhkmnptuvxBCEHPT] ...
#     deploy/hw_probe.sh: line 20: $'\r': command not found
#     deploy/hw_probe.sh: line 22: syntax error near unexpected token `$'in\r''
#
# The carriage return is invisible in an editor, the file looks perfect, and the error names a line
# that has nothing wrong with it. This applies to `bash script.sh` as much as to `./script.sh`: the
# shell is parsing the bytes either way, so the shebang is not what saves you.
#
# Only what a POSIX shell, `make`, a container runtime or a shell-read config file consumes is pinned.
# Python source is not: CPython reads CRLF without complaint, and nothing here is invoked as
# `./file.py`. Markdown and JSON are not: a carriage return there is cosmetic.
#
# Check your own clone with:  git ls-files --eol '*.sh' | grep -v 'i/lf'   (empty = nothing to fix)

# Shell scripts — the joining kit, the node/miner/validator helpers, the container entrypoints.
*.sh                    text eol=lf

# Read line by line by tools that do not strip a carriage return.
Makefile                text eol=lf
Dockerfile*             text eol=lf
docker/Caddyfile        text eol=lf

# Read by a shell and COMPARED as a string: a trailing carriage return makes an equal value unequal,
# which is the failure mode that leaves no error message at all.
docker/CONSENSUS_EPOCH  text eol=lf

# Prose and Python are pinned too, for a different reason than the scripts above: a carriage return
# does not break them (CPython reads CRLF, Markdown does not care), it breaks the DIFF. A contributor
# on Linux who edits one CRLF file sends back a whole-file change, and the review is unreadable. This
# repository is republished from a fresh tree each time, so the rule normalises at check-in rather
# than leaving anyone with a clone that looks modified before they touch it.
*.md                    text eol=lf
*.py                    text eol=lf
*.yml                   text eol=lf
*.json                  text eol=lf
# The chain itself: the mirror ships 140 Go sources and its proto definitions. Unpinned, a clone on a
# machine whose `core.autocrlf` is true receives them with carriage returns, and the two gits that
# read such a tree then disagree about what is modified. That is not theoretical: it happened in the
# tree this mirror is generated from, where it stamped a publication seal `-dirty` on a clean commit.
*.go                    text eol=lf
*.proto                 text eol=lf

# Never end-of-line converted, never diffed as text.
*.png                   binary
*.ico                   binary
*.svg                   text
